POLE: violation. The rule MUST fire and be cited.

MUST-2 is breached: a paths: filter is placed on a workflow that provides a
branch-protection REQUIRED context. On a pull request touching no crate the
workflow never instantiates, so the required context never reports — and
branch protection does not read a context that never reported as passing. It
sits at "Expected" and the pull request cannot merge.

The transcript's own reasoning states the defect in plain words ("if the
workflow does not run there is nothing to fail") and reaches the opposite
conclusion, so a judge cannot clear it on the grounds that the intent was cost
reduction. The sanctioned shape for the same goal is a per-job relevance skip,
which still reports a terminal conclusion.

